WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:03.360
This video was made possible by Hover.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:11.039
Professionalize your web presence with a custom
domain and email address for 10% off at hover.com/Wendover.

00:00:11.039 --> 00:00:16.630
About 13 million times per day, someone clicks
the order button on amazon.com.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:22.430
Some days later, all, or at least almost all,
of those 13 million orders arrive at their

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:23.430
destination.

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.060
But what happens in between?

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:28.970
How does Amazon get a package to you?

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:31.730
Well, it depends… a lot.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:37.520
In fact, Amazon’s fulfillment system, their
shipping system, is more complicated and convoluted

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.390
than that of almost any logistics company.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:46.810
It’s far more complicated than that of UPS,
or FedEx, or DHL, or any other major delivery

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:47.810
company.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:52.440
In a counterintuitive way, this complicated
and convoluted fulfillment system is a crucial

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.980
component of the secret sauce that’s driving
Amazon’s success.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:02.130
They’re striving to make the consumer experience
simple through behind-the-scenes complexity.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:07.909
So, back to the question: how does an Amazon
package get to you, and the answer, it depends.

00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:12.960
It depends first on who’s fulfilling the
package—Amazon or the seller.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.470
About one fourth of sales in the US are fulfilled
directly by the seller, as most products on

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:22.080
Amazon are listed there by a third-party,
which can send packages directly through UPS,

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.680
FedEx, the postal service, or another consumer
delivery company if they choose.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:30.740
Amazon has nothing to do with the fulfillment
of those orders, and the process looks largely

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:33.700
the same as with any other e-commerce company.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:38.251
What’s different is how the other three
quarters of Amazon packages in the US get

00:01:38.251 --> 00:01:42.000
to you—the ones that are fulfilled directly
by Amazon.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.430
The path that these take depends first on
how big the package is.

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:52.060
You see, Amazon’s fulfillment centers are
more-or-less split into three categories:

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:55.630
small sortable, large sortable, and large
non-sortable.

00:01:55.630 --> 00:02:01.130
That first category, small sortable, represents
the bread and butter of Amazon’s business.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:06.579
These are items that are less than 12 by 16
by 6 inches or 30 by 40 by 15 centimeters

00:02:06.579 --> 00:02:10.790
in size, and about 25 pounds or 11 kilograms
in weight.

00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:15.230
The next category, large sortable, is basically
anything larger than this up to a weight of

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:18.010
about 60 pounds or 27 kilograms.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:22.750
Now, the reason for the split between large
and small is because the fulfillment operations

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:28.610
of smaller items is much easier to automate—they
can fit on conveyor belts and automated robots

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:32.140
and other tools that lower the company's reliance
on humans.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:37.030
For example, Amazon uses a robot called the
Kiva which fundamentally changed the way the

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:41.760
job of the company’s pickers, the people
who find and grab an item out of storage,

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:42.760
worked.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:47.580
Previously, pickers would walk some 10 to
12 miles or 16 to 19 kilometers a day through

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.540
cavernous rows of shelves.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:55.070
Now, at least in their most advanced fulfillment
centers, a robot picks up an entire mobile

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:59.780
shelf, on which a required product is located,
and transports it to the picker, who picks

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:00.780
it.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:05.580
Essentially, rather than the picker going
to the shelf, the shelf goes to the picker.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:10.750
With these robots, one person can pick three
to four hundred items an hour rather than

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:13.780
the one hundred or so that was possible on
foot.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:17.800
Of course, fewer humans in the mix is good
for Amazon, given the amount of criticism

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:22.240
it receives for its treatment of workers,
and also because humans, even low-paid ones,

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:23.700
are expensive.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.900
The fulfillment process for larger items,
though, is just tougher to automate cheaply,

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:32.709
so the company chooses to segment the two
processes out, and runs a far more manual

00:03:32.709 --> 00:03:35.920
and distinct fulfillment system for larger
items.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:41.769
However, in most, but not all cases, the fulfillment
centers for large and small items are under

00:03:41.769 --> 00:03:45.550
the same roof, even if they’re operated
completely independently.

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:49.970
Of course, the ideal scenario for Amazon would
be to have every single item they sell in

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:53.440
every single warehouse, but that’s not realistic.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:58.800
Therefore, they use predictive modeling to
try to put items closest to those who are

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.190
likely to buy them.

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:05.379
The US is far from homogeneous, so demand
for different products varies from place to

00:04:05.379 --> 00:04:06.379
place.

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:11.170
For example, in Miami, people probably aren’t
looking to buy many ice scrapers for their

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:12.170
cars.

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:17.249
Meanwhile, in Fargo, North Dakota, demand
for this item is almost certainly quite high.

00:04:17.249 --> 00:04:22.379
It’s therefore no wonder why that, if you
look at the data, the colder the city, the

00:04:22.379 --> 00:04:25.409
faster you can get an ice scraper on Amazon.

00:04:25.409 --> 00:04:28.319
That is Amazon’s predictive stocking at
work.

00:04:28.319 --> 00:04:33.759
While most examples of this system are far
more nuanced and far less intuitive, the concept

00:04:33.759 --> 00:04:38.860
is simple: their algorithms put products closest
to the consumers most likely to buy them—something

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:42.729
only possible at this scale thanks to modern
big-data analytics.

00:04:42.729 --> 00:04:48.639
Of course, there’s then that third category
of products—large non-sortable.

00:04:48.639 --> 00:04:52.830
The distinction here is because Amazon likes
to aggregate products together into as few

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:57.780
packages as possible—unsurprisingly, fewer
packages equals lower costs.

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:03.469
So, both the sortable categories include anything
that could possibly be packaged together in

00:05:03.469 --> 00:05:04.689
a single box.

00:05:04.689 --> 00:05:10.479
The largest items—say a 70 pound beanbag,
for example—are shipped from the large non-sortable

00:05:10.479 --> 00:05:12.139
fulfillment centers.

00:05:12.139 --> 00:05:16.530
These facilities are even less automated than
the large-sortable ones, and even include

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:19.939
workers who create custom boxes for odd-sized
items.

00:05:19.939 --> 00:05:25.430
In Colorado, for example, this is a completely
separate facility, located in Aurora, from

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:28.009
the sortable fulfillment center in Thornton.

00:05:28.009 --> 00:05:34.039
Now, some large items will go directly into
the system of a third-party provider, typically

00:05:34.039 --> 00:05:38.039
XPO Logistics, which would deliver these bulky
items to their final destination, while others

00:05:38.039 --> 00:05:40.860
will continue on in Amazon’s system.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:45.889
The portion of large non-sortable items not
sent to a third party logistics provider,

00:05:45.889 --> 00:05:50.869
plus all the large and small sortable packages
would next be sent to a regional sortation

00:05:50.869 --> 00:05:51.869
center.

00:05:51.869 --> 00:05:56.399
In Colorado, those two fulfillment centers
send their packages to a single sortation

00:05:56.399 --> 00:06:00.219
center, located just minutes away from the
Aurora fulfillment center.

00:06:00.219 --> 00:06:05.770
This is a massive facility, almost half a
million square feet in size, with robots running

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:10.399
around, dropping packages into different chutes,
which each represent a different grouping

00:06:10.399 --> 00:06:11.490
of zip codes.

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:17.729
Now, not all the sortation centers are quite
so automated, but each outputs the same thing—pallets

00:06:17.729 --> 00:06:21.539
of packages going to roughly the same place.

00:06:21.539 --> 00:06:25.289
What happens next, though, once again, depends.

00:06:25.289 --> 00:06:29.830
A package heading to Miami, for example, would
end up on a pallet with other packages for

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:34.589
Miami, which itself would end up on a truck
carrying pallets for Tampa, North Carolina,

00:06:34.589 --> 00:06:39.529
Houston, Baltimore, New York City, Connecticut
and a few other cities and states to the east.

00:06:39.529 --> 00:06:45.029
This truck would then drive the 15 minutes
to a waiting 767 cargo plane at Denver International

00:06:45.029 --> 00:06:48.449
Airport branded in “Prime Air” livery.

00:06:48.449 --> 00:06:54.539
Now, Amazon Air started in 2015 with its lease
of about 20 aircraft from Air Transport International

00:06:54.539 --> 00:06:59.139
and has since grown to almost 70 aircraft—all
leased from other airlines.

00:06:59.139 --> 00:07:04.119
However, the company recently announced the
purchase of their first 11 aircraft—also

00:07:04.119 --> 00:07:09.229
767, bought from Delta and WestJet—meaning
they’ll soon both own and operate their

00:07:09.229 --> 00:07:10.999
own aircraft.

00:07:10.999 --> 00:07:15.389
Those eastbound pallets would all be loaded
around 4:00 am, before the aircraft’s scheduled

00:07:15.389 --> 00:07:17.990
departure time of 4:54 am.

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:23.749
Now, anyone familiar with UPS or FedEx’s
operations will know why this departure time

00:07:23.749 --> 00:07:25.139
is strange.

00:07:25.139 --> 00:07:29.529
If UPS was transporting this package to Miami,
at least at its fastest speed, it would have

00:07:29.529 --> 00:07:34.259
departed on an aircraft the previous night
at 9:40 pm, been flown to Louisville, sorted

00:07:34.259 --> 00:07:38.419
through the company’s hub, then flown to
Miami, arriving at 5:50 am.

00:07:38.419 --> 00:07:41.669
FedEx would have done roughly the same, just
through Memphis instead.

00:07:41.669 --> 00:07:46.550
That’s because FedEx, UPS, and most other
major delivery companies are oriented towards

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:50.610
overnight delivery—they make a big chunk
of their money charging big rates to take

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:55.270
a package from one part of the US one day
and deliver it to another the next day.

00:07:55.270 --> 00:08:00.680
Amazon, meanwhile, built their Prime brand
off of the promise of two-day delivery—ordering

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:03.889
a package on a Monday and getting it on a
Wednesday, for example.

00:08:03.889 --> 00:08:08.479
While they’ve since strayed from the rigidness
of that system, the fastest shipping they’ll

00:08:08.479 --> 00:08:13.350
offer for an item not stocked at a local fulfillment
center is two days, meaning they don’t have

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:16.999
to worry about being able to get a package
from Denver to Miami overnight.

00:08:16.999 --> 00:08:21.749
That’s why UPS and FedEx’s planes take
off in the evening, while Amazon’s leave

00:08:21.749 --> 00:08:22.749
in the morning.

00:08:22.749 --> 00:08:27.809
So, that means it’s about 9:00 am by the
time that Amazon Air plane from Denver gets

00:08:27.809 --> 00:08:30.499
to Cincinnati airport each day.

00:08:30.499 --> 00:08:35.099
Through the morning hours, a dozen or so aircraft
land in Cincinnati, and this timing gives

00:08:35.099 --> 00:08:38.229
the company a major advantage.

00:08:38.229 --> 00:08:43.789
Cincinnati airport is also home to DHL’s
main Americas hub, but DHL, like FedEx and

00:08:43.789 --> 00:08:49.620
UPS, conducts its operations primarily overnight
starting at around midnight, when dozens of

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:53.120
their aircraft from all around the world land
and unload.

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:58.370
Over the next few hours, packages and pallets
are sorted and loaded onto other aircraft,

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:00.790
which all tend to take off by 8:00 am.

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:06.329
That means DHL only really uses their facility
during the overnight hours, so Amazon leases

00:09:06.329 --> 00:09:08.060
it for daytime use.

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:13.310
While Amazon is building their own, larger
facility at Cincinnati airport, this partnership

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:15.269
gave them a huge head-start.

00:09:15.269 --> 00:09:21.250
So, each day, between 8:00 am and 4:00 pm,
Amazon’s there, turning their planes around,

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:25.399
and sorting pallets from where they come from,
like Denver, to where they need to go, like

00:09:25.399 --> 00:09:26.410
Miami.

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:31.339
That Miami flight takes off each weekday at
2:15 pm, and then it lands in Florida just

00:09:31.339 --> 00:09:33.920
before 5:00 pm eastern time.

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:38.529
Now, not every Amazon Air itinerary looks
like this.

00:09:38.529 --> 00:09:42.870
In fact, while UPS and FedEx route almost
all of their flights through their Louisville

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:47.250
and Memphis super-hubs, or through some of
their secondary hubs across the country, only

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:51.690
20% of Amazon Air’s flights go through their
main Cincinnati hub.

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:56.800
That’s because, with the orientation towards
two-day delivery versus one, they just have

00:09:56.800 --> 00:09:58.649
more time.

00:09:58.649 --> 00:10:03.310
The more time means that, in order to service
the entirety of Florida, Amazon only needs

00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:07.730
to fly to three destinations—Miami, Tampa,
and and Lakeland.

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:12.199
That’s because Amazon’s flights to these
airports typically land by 5:00 pm, meaning

00:10:12.199 --> 00:10:16.879
there’s a whole twelve hours before packages
have to be at the local delivery center for

00:10:16.879 --> 00:10:18.579
the final destination.

00:10:18.579 --> 00:10:23.500
The entirety of Florida is within an eight-hour
drive of Lakeland, meaning Amazon can deliver

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:29.000
to all of Florida by only serving effectively
one, but in practice three airports.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:34.670
Meanwhile, FedEx, for example, flies from
Memphis to Fort Lauderdale, Fort Myers, Jacksonville,

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:38.720
Tallahassee, Tampa, and Palm Beach, then,
from Fort Lauderdale, it operates feeder flights

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:41.430
on smaller aircraft to Key West and Marathon.

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:48.199
So, in summary, in order to serve all of Florida,
this is what FedEx has to do and this is what

00:10:48.199 --> 00:10:49.920
Amazon has to do.

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:54.730
The required number of destinations for full
coverage is greatly reduced by orienting for

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:59.240
two-day delivery, and this also means that
Amazon doesn’t have to route every flight

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:01.660
via one main hub.

00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:06.680
Because they operate to far fewer destinations,
it’s much easier for Amazon to fill a plane

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:11.200
from California, for example, exclusively
with packages destined for Florida, since

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:16.350
while a given UPS or FedEx flight carries
packages from or to just one city, a given

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:22.029
Amazon Air flight likely carries packages
from or to a whole state or region.

00:11:22.029 --> 00:11:27.290
Therefore, Amazon Air has flights from all
these airports to Lakeland, meaning that,

00:11:27.290 --> 00:11:31.360
while a package from Denver would have to
route via Cincinnati, one from Los Angeles

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:35.230
or Dallas or Chicago could fly direct.

00:11:35.230 --> 00:11:39.829
With a full hub-and-spoke strategy, like that
of UPS or FedEx, a package would have to be

00:11:39.829 --> 00:11:45.040
loaded and unloaded from aircraft twice, and
sometimes more, while Amazon Air’s nonstop

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:50.220
flights only require loading and unloading
once, which reduces cost, and can fly packages

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:52.640
direct, which also reduces cost.

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:58.699
This is how Amazon can transport packages
by air more efficiently than UPS or FedEx.

00:11:58.699 --> 00:12:03.899
The airplane, however, is just one of five
routes that an Amazon package could take onwards

00:12:03.899 --> 00:12:06.310
from the Denver sortation center.

00:12:06.310 --> 00:12:10.240
For those that are to be delivered locally,
in the Denver area, they’ll be driven to

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:12.829
one of four delivery stations for Denver.

00:12:12.829 --> 00:12:17.379
There, they’ll be loaded into smaller delivery
vans, operated by independent companies or

00:12:17.379 --> 00:12:22.579
individuals contracted by Amazon, which will
take the packages to their final destinations.

00:12:22.579 --> 00:12:27.760
This is the one and only case in which an
Amazon package is handled by Amazon logistics

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:31.810
from start to finish, and it’s generally
how their packages are delivered within major

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:34.220
urban and suburban areas.

00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:38.610
Only a slim majority of their overall package
volume is delivered this way, though, as when

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:43.209
you leave major urban areas, things get a
bit more complicated.

00:12:43.209 --> 00:12:48.470
Now, a package heading outside the Denver
area with a later delivery date, or with a

00:12:48.470 --> 00:12:53.980
destination within the greater Rockies region,
would end up on an Amazon-branded, but independently

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:55.949
operated semi-truck.

00:12:55.949 --> 00:13:01.209
For example, packages to the Aspen area, about
a three to four hour drive into the mountains,

00:13:01.209 --> 00:13:03.180
leave at around 1:00 am each night.

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:08.009
Early in the morning, however, at around 5:00
am, they arrive at the local post office and

00:13:08.009 --> 00:13:12.319
from there, they’re fully transferred into
the United States Postal Service system for

00:13:12.319 --> 00:13:13.540
final delivery.

00:13:13.540 --> 00:13:18.310
You see, in less populous areas, like the
mountains of Colorado, it just doesn’t make

00:13:18.310 --> 00:13:22.029
sense for Amazon to operate their own last-mile
delivery.

00:13:22.029 --> 00:13:26.500
They need a certain amount of scale for that
to be cheaper than the alternative, and, at

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:30.540
least right now, that scale is only possible
in major metro areas.

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:36.339
Therefore, they need alternatives for smaller
cities, towns, and rural areas, and that alternative

00:13:36.339 --> 00:13:38.620
is more often than not the USPS.

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:43.920
That’s because the postal service charges
very low rates for last-mile delivery, assuming

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:47.149
Amazon transports the packages to the local
post office themselves.

00:13:47.149 --> 00:13:53.059
While exact numbers aren’t publicly known,
estimates indicate the USPS charges Amazon

00:13:53.059 --> 00:13:58.240
about $2 per package for last-mile delivery—about
half of what other delivery companies would

00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:00.170
for the same service.

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:05.730
After all, the USPS services every address
in America, so wherever Amazon needs to deliver,

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:08.070
the USPS is going there anyways.

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:13.610
That’s why for smaller places that still
have a decent volume of packages, like mid-sized

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:19.170
towns and cities, USPS delivery is often the
cheapest option for Amazon.

00:14:19.170 --> 00:14:25.399
But then there’s that next step down—the
most rural places in America.

00:14:25.399 --> 00:14:30.319
Everything that can’t be cheaply delivered
by Amazon or the USPS, typically because they’re

00:14:30.319 --> 00:14:34.019
destined for low-density areas where you couldn’t
even fill a truck to send to the local post

00:14:34.019 --> 00:14:39.100
office, or because of capacity or speed reasons,
is sent through UPS.

00:14:39.100 --> 00:14:43.630
Right now, it’s believed that about 20%
of Amazon’s packages end up delivered by

00:14:43.630 --> 00:14:44.649
UPS.

00:14:44.649 --> 00:14:49.339
In Colorado, a package destined for the most
rural areas, like the western edge of San

00:14:49.339 --> 00:14:52.639
Miguel County, for example, would take this
route.

00:14:52.639 --> 00:14:56.579
It would cost Amazon far more, and they’d
likely end up losing money on the cheapest

00:14:56.579 --> 00:15:01.020
items, but it’s what’s required for them
to be able to service every address in the

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:02.920
US quickly.

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:07.540
No matter which of these routes a package
takes, the end result is, at least hopefully,

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:13.759
the same—UPS, the USPS, or Amazon itself
drops off a package at its final destination,

00:15:13.759 --> 00:15:16.199
after a few days of travel across the US.

00:15:16.199 --> 00:15:21.559
It’s a very simple process from a consumer
perspective, but the behind the scenes is

00:15:21.559 --> 00:15:23.360
incredibly complex.

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:28.079
The complexity is what’s needed, though,
for a business who’s whole value proposition

00:15:28.079 --> 00:15:31.740
is to get anything to your door quickly and
cheaply.

00:15:31.740 --> 00:15:37.649
The core of Amazon, their competitive advantage,
is now logistics, to the point where many

00:15:37.649 --> 00:15:42.350
experts believe that the company will start
offering delivery as a service to other companies

00:15:42.350 --> 00:15:43.790
in the coming years.

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:46.990
They believe that they’ve perfected their
system enough that they’re going to take

00:15:46.990 --> 00:15:48.829
on UPS and FedEx.

00:15:48.829 --> 00:15:54.050
Of course, Amazon still has competitors, which
are going after the company as aggressively

00:15:54.050 --> 00:15:55.050
as ever.

00:15:55.050 --> 00:16:00.040
Target, for example, has been able to build
a delivery system with similar speed through

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:03.540
completely different means—ones that are
far simpler.

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:08.459
They’ve essentially turned each of their
1,900 stores across every US state into a

00:16:08.459 --> 00:16:09.769
fulfillment center.

00:16:09.769 --> 00:16:15.559
They send employees out into the aisles who
pick, pack, and ship online orders.

00:16:15.559 --> 00:16:19.809
That means that their packages never have
to travel very far at all, and so they can

00:16:19.809 --> 00:16:24.970
take advantage of cheap and quick ground shipping
from UPS, FedEx, and other more traditional

00:16:24.970 --> 00:16:26.199
providers.

00:16:26.199 --> 00:16:31.249
Some 80% of the company’s orders are fulfilled
directly from its stores and now Walmart is

00:16:31.249 --> 00:16:35.680
also adopting this direct-from-store fulfillment
system as well.

00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:40.379
So far, though, Amazon believes in their system
of simplicity through complexity, and they’re

00:16:40.379 --> 00:16:44.571
making moves to further expand the system
within the US and emulate it in Europe, where

00:16:44.571 --> 00:16:48.680
they recently opened their first air hub in
Leipzig, and put their first two freight aircraft

00:16:48.680 --> 00:16:50.550
online in November, 2020.

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:56.610
UPS, FedEx, and the other major consumer delivery
companies are already well aware of just how

00:16:56.610 --> 00:16:59.509
big a threat Amazon poses to them.

00:16:59.509 --> 00:17:04.530
Amazon has managed to build a 21st-century
logistics network using 21st-century techniques

00:17:04.530 --> 00:17:08.860
and technologies, and, of course, part of
the threat comes from the company’s market

00:17:08.860 --> 00:17:14.510
dominance, but most is because Amazon is innovating
in a way others aren’t.

00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:20.069
The way FedEx and UPS move packages today
is largely the same way they did decades ago,

00:17:20.069 --> 00:17:24.559
but Amazon’s system is only possible through
innovations that allow them to manage such

00:17:24.559 --> 00:17:28.039
a massive, complex, and convoluted system.

00:17:28.039 --> 00:17:32.990
This is all to say: if you’re worried about
Amazon’s growing position as the one-stop-shop

00:17:32.990 --> 00:17:39.029
for the world, be worried, because they’re
only getting better at it.

00:17:39.029 --> 00:17:44.159
What Amazon’s rise proves more than anything
is that, like it or not, online is the new

00:17:44.159 --> 00:17:45.220
main street.

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:49.700
Therefore, just as you’d be concerned with
what your store-front looks like on main street,

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:52.710
you should be concerned about how your web
presence looks.

00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:58.529
Now, in the case of email, having a non-custom
address is the equivalent of having a dirty

00:17:58.529 --> 00:18:00.870
sign—it works, but it’s not pretty.

00:18:00.870 --> 00:18:05.899
That’s why you’ll notice that most professionals
use custom email addresses—something that

00:18:05.899 --> 00:18:07.549
ends in something like @wendover.productions.

00:18:07.549 --> 00:18:13.420
That’s a domain that I bought through Hover,
made possible thanks to their over 400 unique

00:18:13.420 --> 00:18:18.240
domain extensions that let you go way behind
.com, .org, or .net.

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:22.000
After getting that domain, it took about two
